Why the absent mark is safe. A page of the session's own history is written through at the end of the turn that
made it, and a pending write locks its host pages (write_backup_storage, unified_radix_cache.py:2055-2060), so a
span that is missing from memory was either written before it left memory or never: its storage state does not
improve while the turn waits. What can change it: another session writing a shared prefix (the mark then costs a
recompute of what appeared meanwhile), and the SSD cleaner (a later lookup finds less and moves the mark down).
A match that shrinks below absent_from re-opens the lookup, because _shared_chain takes max(absent_from, matched)
only for the "nothing to read" test and the next lookup asks from the new match.

The staged lookup is not an arrival. _stage (next_turn_prefetch.py:1535-1572) calls prefetch_from_storage with the
key's own tail before the turn exists, and the gate runs inside that call. It must neither set shared_tokens (the
tail trivially shares everything with itself) nor price a recompute (F4):
